---
import Layout from "../layouts/Layout.astro";

// A background watermark: a stretch of sheet music (5 staff lines, a
// scatter of notes) that pans sideways as the visitor scrolls. Universally
// recognizable as "music" without needing any theory knowledge, unlike a
// circle of fifths. Hand-placed, not generated — a real staff isn't a
// formula, and the slightly uneven spacing reads more like music than a
// grid would.
const STAFF_LINES = [45, 67.5, 90, 112.5, 135];
const STAFF_NOTES = [
  { x: 30, y: 135 },
  { x: 70, y: 112.5 },
  { x: 110, y: 78.75 },
  { x: 150, y: 90 },
  { x: 190, y: 56.25 },
  { x: 230, y: 67.5 },
  { x: 270, y: 123.75 },
  { x: 310, y: 101.25 },
  { x: 350, y: 45 },
  { x: 390, y: 78.75 },
  { x: 430, y: 112.5 },
  { x: 470, y: 90 },
  { x: 510, y: 67.5 },
  { x: 550, y: 56.25 },
  { x: 590, y: 123.75 },
  { x: 630, y: 101.25 },
  { x: 670, y: 45 },
  { x: 710, y: 78.75 },
  { x: 750, y: 112.5 },
  { x: 790, y: 90 },
  { x: 830, y: 67.5 },
  { x: 870, y: 56.25 },
].map((note) => ({
  ...note,
  // Real notation convention: stems point down from notes above the
  // middle line, up from notes below it.
  stemUp: note.y > 90,
}));
---

<Layout title="Seven Chords">
  <header>
    <nav aria-label="Primary">
      <p class="nav-brand">Seven Chords</p>
    </nav>
  </header>
  <main>
    <section class="hook" aria-label="Introduction">
      <div class="hook-bg" aria-hidden="true">
        <svg class="hook-bg-inner" viewBox="0 0 900 180">
          {
            STAFF_LINES.map((y) => (
              <line x1="0" y1={y} x2="900" y2={y} class="hook-bg-staff" />
            ))
          }
          {
            STAFF_NOTES.map((note) => (
              <g class="hook-bg-note">
                <ellipse
                  cx={note.x}
                  cy={note.y}
                  rx="10.5"
                  ry="8.25"
                  transform={`rotate(-15 ${note.x} ${note.y})`}
                />
                <line
                  x1={note.stemUp ? note.x + 9.75 : note.x - 9.75}
                  y1={note.y}
                  x2={note.stemUp ? note.x + 9.75 : note.x - 9.75}
                  y2={note.stemUp ? note.y - 30 : note.y + 30}
                />
              </g>
            ))
          }
        </svg>
      </div>
      <div class="hook-slide">
        <div class="hook-content">
          <p class="hook-eyebrow">Question time</p>
          <p class="hook-line">What's the one thing you never go a day without?</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="hook-slide alt">
        <div class="hook-content">
          <p class="hook-eyebrow">For me,</p>
          <p class="hook-line">It's music.</p>
          <p class="hook-sub">
            It's in ads, elevators, workouts, breakups — background noise
            nobody thinks about.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="hook-slide">
        <div class="hook-content">
          <p class="hook-eyebrow">And here's the thing —</p>
          <p class="hook-line">You already know this.</p>
          <p class="hook-sub">
            You've felt a chord pull toward home a thousand times. You
            just never had the words for why.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="hook-slide alt">
        <div class="hook-content">
          <p class="hook-eyebrow">So</p>
          <p class="hook-line">Here are the words.</p>
          <p class="hook-sub">Scroll on.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <h1>Chords in context</h1>

    <section class="intro" aria-label="A guided chord progression">
      <div class="intro-sticky">
        <div class="piano-container intro-piano"></div>
        <p class="intro-caption" aria-live="polite"></p>
        <button class="play-chord intro-play" type="button">▶ Play chord</button>
      </div>
      <div class="intro-steps">
        <div class="intro-step" data-degree="0">
          <p>
            This is <strong>I</strong> — home, C major. Everything else in
            the key is defined by its distance from this one.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="intro-step" data-degree="4">
          <p>
            Jump to <strong>V</strong> — G major, the dominant. This is
            tension: the chord built on the note farthest from home,
            pulling hard to resolve.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="intro-step" data-degree="5">
          <p>
            <strong>vi</strong> — A minor, the relative minor. It shares
            every note with I, but casts them in a minor light — home's
            melancholy twin.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="intro-step" data-degree="3">
          <p>
            <strong>IV</strong> — F major, the subdominant. A step away
            from home, setting up one more return.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="intro-step" data-degree="0">
          <p>
            And back to <strong>I</strong>. This exact progression —
            I, V, vi, IV — is behind more pop songs than any other four
            chords in music. Once you hear it, you can't unhear it.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="bridge" aria-label="From one key to every key">
      <p>
        Every one of those roles — home, tension, the relative minor —
        exists in any key, not just C. The same I–V–vi–IV shape works
        everywhere; only the actual notes change.
      </p>
      <p>
        Some keys are close neighbors. Step from C to G and six of the
        seven notes stay the same — only one shifts. Keep stepping and you
        eventually reach F♯, sharing almost nothing with C at all.
      </p>
      <p>
        Arrange every key by that closeness and they fall into a circle —
        neighbors on the wheel sound like each other. That's the map
        below: pick a key, and explore all seven of its chords, including
        the three you haven't heard yet.
      </p>
    </section>

    <div id="app"></div>
  </main>
  <script src="../scripts/parallax.ts"></script>
  <script src="../scripts/intro.ts"></script>
  <script src="../scripts/app.ts"></script>
</Layout>
